# Local development settings. Copy to .env (git-ignored): cp .env.example .env
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Superuser, used only by `pnpm db:setup` (creates the database and logins) and by tests.
DATABASE_ADMIN_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/postgres
# Request-serving login. Row-level security limits it to one shop per transaction.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://hatti_app:hatti_app@localhost:5432/hatti
# Cell-wide jobs such as the outbox relay. Sees every shop; give it only to processes that need it.
DATABASE_SYSTEM_URL=postgres://hatti_system:hatti_system@localhost:5432/hatti
# To run like production, through PgBouncer in transaction mode (see db/pgbouncer/pgbouncer.ini),
# use port 6432 in the three request-serving URLs, and keep the relay's LISTEN on a direct
# connection, since a transaction pooler loses notifications:
# DATABASE_LISTEN_URL=postgres://hatti_system:hatti_system@localhost:5432/hatti
# Staff accounts, credentials and sessions. Only the identity module uses it.
DATABASE_IDENTITY_URL=postgres://hatti_identity:hatti_identity@localhost:5432/hatti

# Keys for secrets at rest, such as authenticator-app seeds and shops' Meta access tokens, which
# the worker opens to send conversions (ADR-143): "id:base64key", newest first.
# This one is for local development only. For any shared environment, generate keys with
#   node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# and keep them in the secret manager.
ENCRYPTION_KEYS=dev:w4lOEQUUBA6nkQTf84p2hmgomQTEky6Cs68n+7oqATw=
# Check new staff passwords against known breaches (Pwned Passwords). Needs internet access; fails
# open when the service is unreachable.
PASSWORD_BREACH_CHECK=true

REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

HOST=0.0.0.0
PORT=4000
# Where customers reach the API's public pages, such as the links that confirm draft orders.
# Required in production; locally the API serves its own links at http://localhost:PORT. The
# worker reads it too, for the links its messages carry (ADR-147).
# PUBLIC_URL=https://hatti.pk
# Where storefronts answer, each at its shop's handle's subdomain (zari.hatti.pk). Required in
# production; locally pnpm dev:storefront serves them at http://{handle}.localhost:4100.
# STOREFRONT_URL=https://hatti.pk
# Where shops point domains of their own with a CNAME record: shops.{STOREFRONT_URL's host}
# unless set. localtest.me lets you try one locally (see getting-started).
# STOREFRONT_DNS_TARGET=shops.hatti.pk
# The key storefronts present to the API's /storefront/ routes, such as carts'. Both the API and
# pnpm dev:storefront read it. This one is for local development only; for any shared
# environment, generate one with
#   node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
STOREFRONT_SERVICE_KEY=dev-storefront-key-for-local-use-only
# Where storefronts reach the API for carts; http://localhost:4000 unless set.
# CORE_API_URL=http://localhost:4000
# Where the theme editor is: only these origins may frame a theme's preview, in design mode, and
# talk to it. Comma-separated; without any, previews have no design mode.
# STOREFRONT_EDITOR_ORIGINS=https://admin.hatti.pk
# Cloudflare, in front of storefronts: the zone their pages are kept in, and a token that may
# purge its cache. The worker purges the pages of what changes; locally there is no edge.
# CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID=
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
# Meta's Graph API, which the worker sends shops' conversions to (ADR-143), and its version; a
# local stand-in can take its place. The worker names shops' storefronts at STOREFRONT_URL.
# META_GRAPH_URL=https://graph.facebook.com
# META_GRAPH_VERSION=v26.0
# How often the worker sends the conversions due, in milliseconds.
# CONVERSIONS_INTERVAL_MS=15000

# Messages to shops' customers about their orders (ADR-146), and the codes merchants sign in with
# (ADR-159). The worker sends shops' WhatsApp templates, and the API sign-in codes, from Hatti's
# shared number through the Cloud API at META_GRAPH_URL: the number's ID and a system user's token.
# The SMS gateway takes a POST of {to, text, sender} with the key as a bearer token. Without them,
# messages and codes go to the log locally; in production messages fail as unsent, and no one can
# sign in by phone. Set up the webhook below with the number too: without its delivery reports,
# every WhatsApp message also goes by SMS after 15 minutes.
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
# WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# SMS_GATEWAY_URL=
# SMS_GATEWAY_KEY=
# SMS_SENDER=Hatti
# How often the worker sends the messages due, in milliseconds.
# MESSAGES_INTERVAL_MS=5000
# Merchants sign up and in with Google (ADR-164) through Hatti's OAuth clients at Google, from the
# Google Cloud console: their IDs, comma-separated, the admin's (a web client) first, then the
# apps'. The API checks Google's ID tokens against the keys Google publishes, so it needs no
# secret. Without them, no one signs in with Google.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_IDS=123456789012-abc.apps.googleusercontent.com
# Hatti's own email about accounts (ADR-165): links proving an account's email and resetting a
# password, sent through Amazon SES's API in its region, with the key of an IAM user allowed
# ses:SendEmail, and ses:GetSuppressedDestination and ses:DeleteSuppressedDestination to lift an
# address from SES's own list as Hatti lifts it (ADR-200), from an address at a domain SES has
# verified. Without them, emails go to the log
# locally; in production no email is proved and no password reset. Their links open the admin at
# ADMIN_URL, the first of PASSKEY_ORIGINS unless set. The worker reads the same settings to email
# customers the news of their orders (ADR-181), from the same address under each shop's name;
# without them, in production, those emails fail as unsent. It emails staff the exports they
# scheduled with them too (ADR-183), the file attached; without them, in production, schedules
# wait.
# SES_REGION=ap-southeast-1
# SES_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# SES_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# EMAIL_FROM=Hatti <no-reply@hatti.pk>
# SES's bounces and complaints (ADR-170): the SNS topic SES publishes them to, subscribed by HTTPS
# to {PUBLIC_URL}/webhooks/ses, which confirms the subscription itself. An address that bounced for
# good, or whose recipient marked an email as spam, is sent no more, until Google confirms one that
# bounced or Hatti's operators lift it (ADR-200).
# SES_FEEDBACK_TOPIC_ARN=arn:aws:sns:ap-southeast-1:123456789012:hatti-ses-feedback
# ADMIN_URL=https://admin.hatti.pk
# Orders booked with shops' own courier accounts (ADR-149, ADR-162): PostEx's and Leopards'
# merchant APIs, which a local stand-in, or Leopards' staging API
# (https://merchantapistaging.leopardscourier.com/api), can take the place of, and how often the
# worker books the bookings due and asks couriers about their parcels, in milliseconds. The worker
# opens the accounts' credentials with ENCRYPTION_KEYS. Outside production, shops can connect the
# test courier too, which books nothing.
# POSTEX_URL=https://api.postex.pk/services/integration/api/order
# LEOPARDS_URL=https://merchantapi.leopardscourier.com/api
# COURIER_BOOKINGS_INTERVAL_MS=30000
# Products' images (ADR-158): how often the worker makes the images due ready, reading the shop's
# uploads or fetching their URLs, and removes those of media gone, in milliseconds. The API serves
# them at {PUBLIC_URL}/images, from the storage below.
# IMAGES_INTERVAL_MS=5000
# Storefront sessions (ADR-180): how often the worker keeps in Postgres the storefronts' counts of
# each day's sessions, kept in Valkey as shoppers' pages tell them, in milliseconds.
# SESSIONS_INTERVAL_MS=60000
# WhatsApp's webhook at {PUBLIC_URL}/webhooks/whatsapp, on the API: the app's secret, which Meta
# signs its requests with, and the verify token given to Meta when subscribing it.
# WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=
# WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=

# Hatti's own Safepay account, which shops pay their plans through (ADR-154): its API key, secret
# key and webhook secret, all three or none, and its environment; its webhook is
# {PUBLIC_URL}/webhooks/billing. Unset, the test gateway takes nothing outside production, and
# invoices are not paid online in production.
# BILLING_SAFEPAY_ENVIRONMENT=production
# BILLING_SAFEPAY_API_KEY=
# BILLING_SAFEPAY_SECRET_KEY=
# BILLING_SAFEPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Where files shops upload are kept (ADR-079). Locally, a directory the API serves at
# {PUBLIC_URL}/storage, signing its URLs with STORAGE_SECRET (a new one at each start unless
# set). In production, R2 through the S3 API. The worker reads the same settings, to remove the
# files of receipts erased with their customer (ADR-113):
# STORAGE_DRIVER=s3
# S3_ENDPOINT=https://{account}.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# S3_BUCKET=hatti-files
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
STORAGE_DRIVER=local
STORAGE_DIRECTORY=.storage
STORAGE_SECRET=dev-storage-secret-for-local-use-only

# Traces and metrics (optional). Start the local stack with
#   docker compose --profile observability up -d
# then uncomment these and open Grafana at http://localhost:3000.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318
# OTEL_METRIC_EXPORT_INTERVAL=10000
